{{also|schande|schände}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|schande}}, from {{inh|de|goh|scanta}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*skandu}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*skandō||shame, disgrace}}. Cognate with {{cog|yi|שאַנדע}}, {{cog|en|shand}}, {{cog|nl|schande}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈʃandə/|aa=Germany}}
** {{audio|de|De-Schande.ogg|aa=Germany}}
* {{IPA|de|/ˈʃandɛ/|[-d̥ɛ]|aa=Austria,South German,Switzerland}}
* {{hyph|de|Schan|de}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|f.sg}}

# [[disgrace]], [[infamy]], [[dishonour]]
#: {{syn|de|Schmach|Beschämung|Entehrung|Unwürdigkeit}}

====Usage notes====
* {{m+|de||Schande}} can overlap with {{m+|en|shame}} insofar as the latter is used in the sense of "disgrace". However, the two words should not be identified generally because this will lead to misunderstandings. Particularly, the phrase {{m|de||es ist eine Schande}} means "it's a disgrace". The German translation for "it's a shame" would be {{m|de||es ist [[schade]]}}.

